# Cultural Landscape Report for Gettysburg National Cemetery (Auwaerter et al.; Olmsted Center for Landscape Preservation, National Park Service; 2017): the site of the future cemetery as it stood in July 1863
# Chapter 'Site History, to 1863' (PDF pp. 43-49) and its Drawing 1.1 (PDF p. 54), looked at as a page image. The site: 22 acres of four fenced fields and part of an orchard north and west of Evergreen Cemetery on Cemetery Hill.
# The default text variant of this source is the content-stream-order text (the standard text interleaves the two columns). Printed page numbers are the numbers at the head of the page.
# The report names its own basis (property records, soldiers' accounts, photographs, the Warren map and the park's 1863 conditions map of 2006); where it writes 'probably', 'may' or 'presumed' the record is hedged.
@ NPS-CLR-CEMETERY
%images 54

54/- | general | Drawing 1.1: the site in July 1863 after the battle, with the fences, fields, orchard, pond and the infantry and artillery lines | - | 1863-07 (a plan drawn in 2016 from the park's 1863 conditions map, the Warren map and monuments; features at approximate location) | stated | The report's drawing of the site before the cemetery plots the landscape in July 1863 after the battle, giving the fence types as 5-rail and Virginia worm, vertical plank and horizontal plank, wood picket and stone, the note that most wood fences were taken down during the battle, and that property boundaries are presumed to correspond with fence lines (a page image was looked at; no geometry is read from it here) | Plan shows landscape in July 1863 after the Battle of Gettysburg. >>> Property boundaries presumed to correspond with fence lines.
54/- | fences | Drawing 1.1 labels: worm fence on a stone fence along Taneytown Road at the Weirick field; plank fence on a stone fence on Baltimore Pike; stone and wood fences disassembled in 1863 | - | 1863-07 (labels of a plan drawn in 2016) | stated | The drawing labels a worm fence on a stone fence (along Taneytown Road at the Weirick field), a plank fence on a stone fence (on Baltimore Pike by the Menchy field), stone and wood fences disassembled in 1863, stone wall traverses presumed in the Pierce field, a breastwork of 1863 on Baltimore Pike, and presumed field entrances | Worm fence on stone fence Plank fence on stone fence
44/25 | fields | The 22-acre site in July 1863: four fenced fields (Menchy, Pierce, Benner, Weirick) and part of the Meyers orchard between Baltimore Pike, Taneytown Road and Steinwehr (Emmitsburg) Road | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that in July 1863 the 22-acre site of the national cemetery, with the annex, comprised four fenced fields and a portion of an orchard north and west of Evergreen Cemetery, between Taneytown Road, Steinwehr Avenue (then the Emmitsburg Road) and Baltimore Pike: the Menchy field and orchard on the Meyers property fronting Baltimore Pike, the Pierce field at the juncture of the Emmitsburg and Taneytown roads, and the Benner and Weirick fields between Evergreen Cemetery and Taneytown Road | #s1-2
45/26 | fences | The Menchy field, about four acres with 250 feet on Baltimore Pike, enclosed by the Meyers orchard and a wood and stone fence perhaps four feet tall | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says the Menchy field was a rectangular field of about four acres with 250 feet of frontage on Baltimore Pike, enclosed by the Meyers orchard along its northeast side and by a wood and stone fence along Baltimore Pike that was perhaps four feet tall | #s2-3
45/26 | fences | The Pierce field, about seven acres: stone walls on the north, west and south sides, the Meyers orchard on the southeast, wood fences on the rest | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the Pierce field, a trapezoidal field of approximately seven acres corresponding with the northern part of the cemetery and the annex, was defined by stone walls on the north, west and south sides, the Meyers orchard along the southeast side and wood fences on the remaining sides | #s4
45/26 | fences | The Benner field, about six acres: wood fences on three sides, a stone fence along Taneytown Road | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the Benner field in the middle of the site encompassed approximately six acres bordered by wood fences on three sides and a stone fence along Taneytown Road | #s5
45/26 | fences | The Weirick field, about four acres: stone and wood fences on the west and south, a wood fence on the north, the grove and a wood fence along Evergreen Cemetery on the east | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the Weirick field at the south end contained approximately four acres bordered by stone and wood fences on its west and south sides, a wood fence on the north, and the grove of trees and a wood fence along Evergreen Cemetery on the east | #s6
45/26 | fields | The fields were open, with no trees or buildings (one exception); hayfield or pasture or cultivated crops; each used militarily July 1-3 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says there were no trees, buildings with one exception, or other features in the fields that interrupted their open character, that the land had been used for agriculture since the late eighteenth century as hayfield, pasture or cultivated crops apart from the Meyers property, and that between July 1 and 3 each field was used with artillery on the upper elevations and south end and infantry along the stone walls on the lower northern parts | There were no trees, buildings (with one exception) >>> and infantry along the stone walls on the lower northern parts of the site.
45/26 | orchards | The Meyers property: fruit growing in 1863 (John and Ester Meyers); the orchard north of the lines of the 75th Pennsylvania and 82nd Ohio, probably damaged | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the southern part of the Meyers property, the area north of the existing cemetery drive, was used for fruit growing in 1863, owned by John and Ester Meyers, that the Meyers orchard was north of the lines of the 75th Pennsylvania and the 82nd Ohio, and that while not in the direct line of fire the orchard undoubtedly received some damage | #s10-12
45/26 | fields | The Menchy field was used for growing grass or grain in 1863 (owner Edward Menchy, who bought it in 1857) | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the Menchy field was used for growing grass or grain in 1863, owned by Edward Menchy who had acquired the property on April 1, 1857 | #s13-14
46/27 | fields | The Pierce field was used for growing grass or grain in 1863 (owners James and Margaret Pierce, who also owned the Dobbin house) | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the Pierce field was used for growing grass or grain in 1863, owned by James and Margaret Pierce, who also owned the farmland across Steinwehr Avenue that included the old Dobbin house built in 1776 | #s13-14
46/27 | fields | The Benner field was used to grow wheat and corn in 1863 (owner Jacob Benner) | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the Benner field was used to grow wheat and corn in 1863 and was owned by Jacob Benner of Baltimore Street, who also owned a farm along the Emmitsburg Road that he sold to George and Dorothy Rose shortly before the war | #s21-23
47/28 | fields | The Weirick field, owned by John Weirick, was used for growing corn in 1863 | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the Weirick field, owned by John Weirick, was used for growing corn in 1863, and that four artillery batteries were stationed on or immediately adjacent to it | #s9-10
47/28 | fences | A post and rail fence divided the Benner and Weirick fields; Huntington's battery most likely straddled it | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says Huntington's battery most likely straddled the boundary between the Benner and Weirick fields, which were divided by a post and rail fence | #s3
46/27 | earthworks | Infantry along the stone walls of the Menchy and Pierce fields and along Taneytown Road on July 2 and 3 | - | 1863-07-02 to 1863-07-03 | stated | The report places the 75th Pennsylvania along the stone wall bordering the Meyers orchard, the 61st Ohio along the stone wall bordering the Pierce field, the 74th Pennsylvania along the stone wall in the western part of the Menchy field bordering the Pierce field, and the 73rd Ohio along the stone wall on Taneytown Road adjoining the Benner field | #s7-11 | #s19
47/28 | roads | Circulation in 1863: dirt farm roads through openings in the stone fences; the Menchy field entered at the opening on Baltimore Pike where the cemetery entrance is | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says circulation within the site in July 1863 consisted of dirt farm roads, openings in the stone fences with dirt tracks or undefined areas through the fields, that the road into the Menchy field was at an opening in the stone and wood fence along Baltimore Pike corresponding to the existing entrance, and that the specific locations of the openings of the other fields are not known | #s24-26
47/28 | general | The Cemetery Hill signal station on the highpoint of the Weirick field | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The report says that on the highpoint within the Weirick field the Union Signal Corps established the Cemetery Hill signal station, where flags were waved to transmit orders | #s23
44/25 | roads | The site bordered by unsurfaced public roads and a brick sidewalk to Evergreen Cemetery; the buildings to the north not destroyed | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that at the time of the battle the site was bordered by unsurfaced public roads, a brick sidewalk leading from town to Evergreen Cemetery along Baltimore Pike, and several brick and frame buildings to the north around the intersection of Baltimore Pike and Steinwehr Avenue, none of which had been destroyed during the battle | #s3-4
44/25 | buildings | Buildings around the site: the Meyers house (about 1837) with its orchard, the Study house (about 1800), the stone Dobbin house of the Pierces | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that at the northeastern side of the site was the approximately two-acre Meyers property with its orchard and a brick house built about 1837, with the Study house of about 1800 to its north, and that across the street was the eighteenth-century stone Dobbin house owned since 1861 by James and Margaret Pierce | #s5-8
44/25 | buildings | The Bishop farm: two fields along Taneytown Road and a farmhouse on the Emmitsburg Road with two barns; a Duttera house; no buildings on the Ziegler fields | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that across Taneytown Road to Emmitsburg Road were the Bishop and Ziegler farms and the Devon house, that the Bishop farm included two fields along Taneytown Road and a farmhouse along Emmitsburg Road with two barns, that a house occupied by the Duttera family was on the southern Bishop field, and that there were no buildings on the Ziegler fields near the site | #s11-14
44/25 | cemetery | Evergreen Cemetery in 1863: young trees, about 500 monuments and headstones, 60 family plots with iron fences, the pumphouse gazebo and the brick gatehouse | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says Evergreen Cemetery was the most prominent part of the landscape around the site, with numerous young trees, an estimated 500 marble and granite monuments and headstones, 60 family plots enclosed by ornamental iron fences, a fanciful pumphouse gazebo and the prominent brick gatehouse on Baltimore Pike, much of it damaged in the fighting | #s15
44/25 | woodlots | A mature grove on the southeast side of the hill, perhaps a fragment of the native forest, most likely oak, ash and hickory | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says that on the southeastern side of the southern hill, extending east from the border of Evergreen Cemetery toward Taneytown Road, was a mature grove of trees, perhaps a fragment of the native forest or an old second-growth woodlot, most likely oak, ash and hickory | #s21-22
44/25 | general | A probably spring-fed pond with a wetland at the foot of the hill in the Pierce field | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says that at the foot of Cemetery Hill within the Pierce field near Steinwehr Avenue, south of the Dobbin house, was a pond that was most likely spring-fed, a natural retention basin surrounded by wetland that drained north under Steinwehr Avenue | #s23-25
48/29 | earthworks | A bank four feet high outside the stone fence on the west side of the Pierce field; infantry probably dug trenches behind the fences | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says there were no known topographic features in the site in July 1863 except a bank outside the stone fence along the west side of the Pierce field, which rose upwards of 4 feet above Taneytown Road and was probably made when the road was built or improved, and that Union infantry probably dug trenches behind the fences and piled the earth against their fronts | #s1-3
48/29 | orchards | The Meyers apple orchard, 'an orchard of gnarled old leafy trees', probably evenly spaced nursery trees about 30 feet apart, a cover for infantry and artillery | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says that other than crops the only known vegetation in the site by July 1863 was the Meyers apple orchard, described by a veteran as an orchard of gnarled old leafy trees, that served as cover for infantry and artillery, that it may have followed advances in fruit growing with nursery-grown trees generally about 30 feet apart, and that it probably included several varieties of apple | #s4-6
48/29 | buildings | Only one building on the site in July 1863: an unidentified shed or farm building near the southwest corner of the Pierce field | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says there was just one building within the site in July 1863, an unidentified shed or other farm building near the southwest corner of the Pierce field | #s7
48/29 | fences | The stone fences of the site: dry-laid walls of field stone, two to three feet high, some topped with wood rails to hold livestock | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the structures within the site were limited to stone fences along the road frontages and some internal boundaries, vernacular dry-laid walls of stones probably gathered in field clearing, mostly low, around two to three feet high, and some topped by wood fence rails to give the height needed to restrain livestock | #s8-10
48/29 | earthworks | Troops used some of the stone fences as breastworks; the north wall of the Menchy field extended into Baltimore Pike; stone traverses presumably in the Pierce field | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says that during the battle troops used some of the stone fences as defensive breastworks, that the stone wall along the north side of the Menchy field was extended out into Baltimore Pike probably by the 82nd Ohio, and that in the Pierce field troops may have built perpendicular stone extensions on the fences along Steinwehr Avenue and Taneytown Road as traverses | #s11-13
48/29 | earthworks | A stone breastwork behind the wall near the 55th Ohio monument; a half-moon barricade of stone about four feet high for Colonel Gambee and staff | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The report says there was also a stone breastwork behind the wall near the present site of the 55th Ohio monument, and quotes Andrew Sweetland of the 55th Ohio that the troops built a barricade of stone about four feet high, in a half-moon shape, a few feet in rear of his company, for the protection of Colonel Gambee and staff | #s16-17
49/30 | fences | Fence types around the fields: five-rail, Virginia worm, vertical plank, horizontal plank and picket; most taken down in the battle, leaving the posts | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says there were several types of fences around the fields of the site, five-rail (five horizontal rails on wood posts), Virginia worm (stacked rails in a zigzag), vertical plank, horizontal plank and picket, that most fences were probably taken down during the battle leaving only the upright posts of the five-rail, plank and picket fences, and that the rails may have been used to reinforce breastworks | Small-scale features within the national cemetery site in July 1863 >>> The rails may have been used to reinforce breastworks.
42/23 | fences | Troops tore down wood and stone fences to remove obstacles and reconfigured stone fences, such as across Baltimore Pike, as breastworks; lunette earthworks at the artillery positions opposite Evergreen Cemetery | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that in the Raffensperger fields across from Evergreen Cemetery troops constructed lunette-shaped earthworks at artillery positions, and that in the fields and along the roads troops tore down wood and stone fences to remove obstacles and built up and reconfigured stone fences, such as along and across Baltimore Pike, as defensive breastworks | In the Raffensperger fields across from Evergreen Cemetery >>> as defensive breastworks (figs. 1.25, 1.26).
42/23 | hospitals | The Meyers house on Baltimore Pike was purportedly used as a hospital and may have been General Howard's headquarters; sharpshooters used the Study house | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The report says that sharpshooters commandeered houses including the Study house on Baltimore Pike, and that the neighboring Meyers house was purportedly used as a hospital and may have served as General Howard's headquarters for the 11th Corps | #s4-5
42/23 | fences | Photographs of July 1863: damaged stone and wood fences along Taneytown Road; stone fences on Baltimore Pike reconfigured as breastworks, one breastwork across the brick sidewalk into the Pike | - | 1863-07 (photographs: Library of Congress cwpb-00887 and LC-B815-273) | stated | The report's captions of two photographs of July 1863 say they show damaged stone and wood fences along Taneytown Road south of Evergreen Cemetery and stone fences along Baltimore Pike reconfigured as breastworks, with a stone breastwork at left center extending across the brick sidewalk into Baltimore Pike along the farm road into the Menchy field | Damaged stone and wood fences along Taneytown Road south of Evergreen Cemetery, July 1863. >>> corresponding with the existing entrance to the national cemetery.
42/23 | cemetery | General Howard had headstones laid flat on the ground; larger monuments served as shelter among the batteries | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says that General Howard ordered troops to lay the headstones flat on the ground to prevent their destruction and that the larger monuments purportedly served as breastworks for troops and batteries actually among the graves | General Howard ordered troops to lay the headstones flat >>> actually among the graves and monuments, which they used for shelter from the enemy’s fire.
43/24 | damage | Battle damage in Evergreen Cemetery: bullet-marked stones, damaged plot fences, planks removed from the fences along Baltimore Pike, the gatehouse hit; fifteen buried by the pumphouse | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says battle damage in Evergreen Cemetery included bullet holes and cracks in grave markers, damage to grave plot fences and removal of planks from the fences along Baltimore Pike, with many young ornamental shade trees still standing, that the gatehouse had smashed windows and holes in its brick walls, and that fifteen were buried alongside the cemetery pumphouse | Battle damage in Evergreen Cemetery included bullet holes >>> Fifteen were buried alongside the cemetery pumphouse.
43/24 | damage | After July 3 the site was a destroyed agricultural landscape; at least one regiment held its position until July 5; broken fences and torn-up fields | - | 1863-07-03 to 1863-07-05 | stated | The report says that after the fighting ceased on July 3 the site was a destroyed agricultural landscape like the rest of the battlefield, with heavy artillery fire and infantry defenses, and that at least one regiment held its position until July 5 while others retreated at once, leaving behind broken fences, clothing and supplies, damaged buildings and torn-up fields | After fighting ceased on July 3, 1863 >>> damaged buildings, and torn-up fields.
%basis Frank Haskell, aide-de-camp, written shortly after the fighting ended (the report's footnote 48)
43/24 | damage | Haskell: the ripening grain, corn, orchards and meadows of the morning trampled, the orchards splintered, the fences prostrate, the harvest trodden in the mud | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report quotes Frank Haskell's account written shortly after the fighting that the ripening grain, the luxuriant corn, the orchards, the grassy meadows and the rural cottages were trampled by countless feet, plowed and scored by shot and shell, the orchards splintered, the fences prostrate and the harvest trodden in the mud | …the ripening grain, the luxuriant corn, the orchards, the grassy meadows >>> the harvest trodden in the mud.

# The same report, chapter 'Site History, 1863-1872' (PDF pp. 57-83): what was built on the site after the battle and when (later features: never drawn in the July 1863 scene).
%images -
%basis -
69/50 | orchards | A photograph of fall 1863 or early 1864 shows part of the Meyers orchard behind the reinterment work in the Pennsylvania plot | - | 1863-10 to 1864-03 (photograph of the reinterments; the orchard of 1863 still stood) | stated | The report's caption of a photograph of the reinterment work in the Pennsylvania plot, looking northeast, says a portion of the Meyers orchard is visible in the background in fall 1863 or early 1864 | Reinterment work underway in the Pennsylvania plot >>> fall 1863 or early 1864.
70/51 | burials | Reinterment of 3,512 Union bodies from the battlefield into the cemetery, completed March 18, 1864 | - | !1863-10 to 1864-03 (reinterments; the contract began October 27, 1863) | stated | The report says that reinterment of the total 3,512 bodies was completed on March 18, 1864 | #s3
66/47 | later | William Saunders completed the working plan of the cemetery landscape in the fall of 1863 | - | !1863-fall (the plan of the national cemetery; nothing of it existed in July) | stated | The report says that during the fall of 1863 William Saunders completed a detailed working plan of the cemetery landscape showing the burial area, walks, roads, the central monument location, the keeper's lodge and the plantings | #s4
73/54 | later | After the battle the Evergreen Cemetery Association rebuilt the white-painted plank fence along Baltimore Pike and repaired the gatehouse with a two-story frame addition | - | !1863-1864 (repairs and reconstruction after the battle) | stated | The report says the Evergreen Cemetery Association probably made initial repairs for the dedication of November 19, 1863 and soon followed with the reconstruction of the white-painted plank fence along Baltimore Pike and the repair of the gatehouse, patching the brick walls and building a two-story frame addition on the rear | #s12
77/58 | later | The cemetery enclosure wall, 2,500 feet of mortared gray Gettysburg granite with a rubble and clay core, substantially complete by the end of 1864 | - | !1864 (enclosure wall of the national cemetery; later than the battle) | stated | The report says the enclosure wall, substantially completed by the end of 1864, extended along the north, west and south sides of the cemetery from Baltimore Pike to Taneytown Road and back to the southwest corner of Evergreen Cemetery for a total of 2,500 feet, connected with the stone wall of Evergreen's southern boundary, and was built of mortared irregularly coursed gray Gettysburg granite with a rubble stone and clay core on a two-foot deep stone foundation | The enclosure wall was substantially completed by the end of 1864 >>> and a two-foot deep stone foundation.
78/59 | later | A three-foot tall, 1,726-foot pipe-rail fence of wrought iron pipe on cast iron posts between the two cemeteries, built and planted with an arborvitae hedge in 1864 | - | !1864 (pipe-rail fence on the Evergreen boundary; the later photograph is of about 1908) | stated | The report says the three-foot tall, 1,726-foot long fence, cast in Philadelphia, consisted of 2 1/2 inch wrought iron pipes supported by square cast iron beveled posts, and its caption says the pipe-rail fence and arborvitae hedge along the Evergreen Cemetery boundary were built and planted in 1864 | This three-foot tall, 1,726-foot long fence, cast in Philadelphia, >>> square cast iron beveled posts | Later photograph of the pipe-rail fence and declining arborvitae hedge along the Evergreen Cemetery boundary built and planted in 1864, photographed ca. 1908.
78/59 | later | The ornamental front fence and entrance gates on Baltimore Pike installed in the spring of 1865 by Wood & Perot of Philadelphia | - | !1865-spring | stated | The report says the contract for the more ornamental front fence and entrance gates along Baltimore Pike, installed in the spring of 1865, was awarded to Wood & Perot Ornamental Iron Works of Philadelphia | The contract for the more ornamental front fence and entrance gates >>> Wood & Perot Ornamental Iron Works of Philadelphia
78/59 | later | Evergreen Cemetery installed the identical iron fence on the northern part of its Baltimore Pike front, replacing the plank fence rebuilt after the battle | - | !1865 (iron fence of Evergreen Cemetery; the plank fence rebuilt after the battle was kept south of the gatehouse) | stated | The report says that soon after the national cemetery fence and gates were completed Evergreen Cemetery had the identical fence installed along the northern part of its Baltimore Pike frontage to replace the white-painted plank fence reconstructed soon after the battle, and that it left the plank fence in place south of its gatehouse | Soon after the national cemetery fence and gates were completed >>> had been reconstructed soon after the battle.
76/57 | later | December 1864: the enclosure wall and lodge almost complete; spring 1865: front fence being installed, lodge and stone wall complete | - | !1864-12 to 1865-spring | stated | The report says that in December 1864 David Wills reported that the enclosure wall and lodge, with a fence between the two cemeteries, were almost complete, and that by the spring of 1865 the front fence was being installed and the lodge and stone wall were complete | In December 1864, David Wills reported >>> the lodge and stone wall were complete (fig. 1.50).
79/60 | avenues | The main drive of the national cemetery completed in stages by 1866 | - | !1866 | stated | The report says the main drive, which Saunders designed as a closed loop through the cemetery, was completed in stages by 1866 | #s8
82/63 | monuments | Soldiers' National Monument: $40,000 appropriated in June 1864; the Batterson design chosen by late 1864 or early 1865; completed 1869 | - | !1864-1869 (the monument; nothing of it stood in July 1863) | stated | The report says the cemetery commissioners, with $40,000 approved in June 1864, solicited designs for the National Monument, later the Soldiers' National Monument, that by late 1864 or early 1865 the board had selected the design of Batterson's Monumental Works of Hartford for a shaft with figural sculptures, and that the drives were probably not finished until after the national monument was completed in 1869 | With the appropriation of $40,000 approved in June 1864 >>> representing Liberty, War, History, Peace, and Plenty | #s4

# The same report, chapter 'Site History, to 1863' before the battle (PDF pp. 34-41): the farm landscape around Cemetery Hill and Evergreen Cemetery as it stood in June 1863.
34/15 | fences | Farms of the first half of the nineteenth century in Adams County: pastures and fields enclosed by stone and rail fences, woodlots, a cluster of buildings by a road | - | pre-1863 (first half of the nineteenth century) | stated | The report says that agriculture in the county in the first half of the nineteenth century was characterized by small market farms producing livestock and grains, and that the typical farm had pastures and cultivated fields enclosed by stone and rail fences, woodlots and a cluster of farm buildings, often along a public road | Agriculture in the county during the first half of the nineteenth century >>> often along a public road
35/16 | general | Gettysburg in 1860: a compact town of 2,390 residents surrounded by farmland | - | 1860 | stated | The report says that by 1860 Gettysburg had developed into a compact urban town of 2,390 residents, characterized by attached brick and frame townhouses, stores and churches, surrounded by farmland | #s3
36/17 | fences | The pastures and fields of the lots around Cemetery Hill (Study, Menchy, Pierce, Benner, Weirick) were lined by stone fences of Gettysburg granite or plank or rail fences, sometimes on top of low stone fences | - | 1863-06 (the lots of the early 1860s) | stated | The report says that by the early 1860s the largest lots in the triangular area used at least in part for agriculture included parts of the Study and Menchy lots, part of the Pierce property and two outlots along Taneytown Road owned by Jacob Benner and John Weirick, and that the pastures and fields on these lots were lined by stone fences constructed of weathered stones of Gettysburg granite, or wood plank or rail fences, which were sometimes added on top of low stone fences | By the early 1860s, the largest lots within the triangular area >>> sometimes added on top of low stone fences
36/17 | buildings | Across Taneytown Road and Baltimore Pike the fields were small town outlots or parts of larger farms; only the Bishop farm had a small barn and outbuilding on Taneytown Road | - | 1863-06 | stated | The report says that across Taneytown Road and Baltimore Pike were agricultural fields on small town outlots or parts of larger farms, most with no farmhouses or barns near Cemetery Hill except the Bishop Farm to the west, which had a small barn and outbuilding along Taneytown Road | #s6-7
36/17 | later | The wall and evergreens bordering Taneytown Road in the 1866 photograph of the Bishop farm were added after the battle | - | !1863-1866 (wall and evergreens along Taneytown Road, added after the battle) | stated | The report's caption of a photograph of about 1866 showing the Bishop farm and barn across Taneytown Road says the photograph shows the overall pre-war character and that the wall and evergreens bordering Taneytown Road were added after the battle | #s13-14
38/19 | fields | Evergreen Cemetery's land: about seventeen acres of field and pasture with one grove at the southwest corner, from the farms of George Shryock and Conrad Snyder | - | 1853-1854 (the land of the cemetery chosen in 1853, laid out 1854) | stated | The report says the site of Evergreen Cemetery recommended in December 1853 as the first hill on the Baltimore turnpike, west side, comprised approximately seventeen acres of field and pasture with one grove of trees at the southwest corner, part of the farms of George Shryock and Conrad Snyder | #s3-4
39/- | cemetery | Evergreen Cemetery in June 1863: hundreds of monuments, iron-fenced family plots, the rocky centre, a temporary white plank fence and gate on the road, a pump gazebo of about 1860 | - | 1863-06 | stated | The report says improvements within Evergreen Cemetery through June 1863 included hundreds of marble and granite grave monuments, some grouped in family plots surrounded by ornamental iron fences, the rocky debris of excavation dumped in the small circular avenue called the rocky centre, a temporary white-painted wood plank fence and entrance gate on the road frontage in place of the planned iron front, and by about 1860 a decorative wooden gazebo with lattice walls enclosing the pump | #s6-9
